[00:01:13.708] Browser LOG     Frontend: Data received from API: {"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","source_language":"auto","target_language":"en","translated_text":"History of Myanmar\n\nMyanmar (English: Myanmar), officially the Republic of the Union of Myanmar (English: Republic of the Union of Myanmar), is a country in Southeast Asia. It shares borders with Bangladesh and India to the west and northwest, the People's Republic of China to the northeast, and Laos and Thailand to the east and southeast, while the Andaman Sea and the Bay of Bengal lie to its south and southwest. The country's capital is Naypyidaw, and its commercial capital is Yangon. [1]\n\nThe earliest settlements in Myanmar were in the Pyu city-states and Mon kingdoms. [8] Around the 9th century AD, the Bamar people established the Bagan Kingdom near the Irrawaddy River, which saw the emergence of Burmese culture, language, and so on. The First Myanmar Empire, Bagan, fell due to Mongol invasions. In the 16th century, the Second Myanmar Empire, the Toungoo Empire, emerged and briefly stood as the largest empire in Southeast Asian history. [9] In the 19th century, the Third Myanmar Empire, the Konbaung Empire, ruled over not only the areas of modern-day Myanmar but also the regions of Assam and Manipur. The British Empire launched invasion wars and conquered Myanmar. During World War II, Myanmar was briefly occupied by the Japanese and then recaptured by the Allies. In 1948, Myanmar gained independence from the British.\n\nMyanmar's post-independence history has been filled with ongoing instability and conflicts to this day. The 1962 military coup led to a military dictatorship under the Burma Socialist Programme Party. Subsequently, the 8888 Uprising on August 8, 1988, led to a transition to a nominal multi-party system two years later; however, the country's post-crisis military council refused to transfer power and continues to rule the nation to this day. The country is fragmented by ethnic conflicts among numerous ethnic groups and is facing one of the world's longest-running civil wars. The United Nations and many other organizations have reported continuous and systematic human rights violations within the country. [10] Following the 2010 general election, the military government was officially dissolved in 2011, and a nominal civilian government was established. Daw Aung San Suu Kyi and political prisoners were released, and the 2015 general election was held, which led to improved foreign relations and the easing of economic sanctions. [11] However, the country's treatment of ethnic minorities, especially in connection with the Rohingya conflict, continued to be a source of international tension and concern. [12] Following the 2020 Myanmar general election, in which Daw Aung San Suu Kyi's party won a majority in both houses of parliament, the Tatmadaw (military) once again seized power in a military coup. [13] The coup, which was widely condemned by the international community, led to widespread and ongoing protests in Myanmar, marked by severe political repression by the military and a further escalation of the civil war. [14] The Tatmadaw arrested Daw Aung San Suu Kyi to remove her from public view and charged her with criminal offenses ranging from corruption to violations of COVID-19 regulations; all charges against her are, according to independent observers, \"politically motivated.\" [15]\n\nMyanmar is a member of the East Asia Summit, the Non-Aligned Movement, ASEAN, and BIMSTEC, but despite once being part of the British Empire, it is not a member of the Commonwealth of Nations. Myanmar is a dialogue partner of the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation. The country is very rich in natural resources such as jade, gemstones, oil, natural gas, teak, and other minerals, and also possesses renewable energy potential with the highest solar energy prospects compared to other countries in the Greater Mekong Subregion. [16] However, Myanmar has long suffered from instability, political violence, corruption, poor infrastructure, and a long history of colonial exploitation that disregarded human development. [17] In 2013, the country's nominal GDP was US$56.7 billion, and its GDP (PPP) was US$221.5 billion. [18] Income inequality in Myanmar is one of the widest in the world, with a large part of the economy controlled by cronies of the military regime. [19] Myanmar is one of the least developed countries. [20] Since 2021, over 600,000 people across Myanmar have been displaced due to the post-coup civil war, and more than three million people are in urgent need of humanitarian assistance. [21] According to the UNHCR, as of December 2024, there are over 1.3 million refugees and asylum-seekers, and 3.5 million internally displaced persons. [22]\n\nTerminology\n\nMain article: Names of Myanmar\n\nIn 1989, the military government decided to change the name from Burma to Myanmar in English.\n\nWestern countries, not recognizing the military government, believe it has no authority to officially change the country's name in English. [23] Therefore, while they accept Myanmar in the Burmese language, they continue to use Burma in English. When speaking English, users choose between \"Burma\" or \"Myanmar,\" and technically, these terms are interchangeable.\n\nMajor international news agencies such as the BBC, Wall Street Journal, and Financial Times, as well as the governments of the United States and Great Britain, continue to refer to the country as Burma. However, CNN, The Economist, and The New York Times have adopted the term Myanmar for the country and the English word \"Burmese\" as the adjective. [24][25]\n\nThe governments of Great Britain, Canada, Australia, and the United States continue to use Burma. [24][26] However, the European Union uses both Burma/Myanmar. [27] China does not use Myanmar but instead uses its traditional term Miǎndiàn (缅甸). Japan refers to the country as Myanmar (ミャンマー) but continues to use Burmese (ビルマ人) for its people. France, like most other countries, uses the previously established translation and calls the country Birmanie. [28][29]\n\nIn 2015, after the new government came to power, Daw Aung San Suu Kyi, the Union Minister for Foreign Affairs, met with ambassadors, military attachés, and consuls from various foreign embassies based in the country in Naypyidaw and stated that it does not matter whether it is called Myanmar or Bama (Burma). [30]","usageMetadata":{"candidatesTokenCount":1391,"promptTokenCount":3062,"promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":3062}],"thoughtsTokenCount":5458,"totalTokenCount":9911}}
