[01:24:44.041] Browser LOG     Frontend: Data received from API: {"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","source_language":"auto","target_language":"en","translated_text":"History of Myanmar\n\nMyanmar (English: Myanmar), officially the Republic of the Union of Myanmar (English: Republic of the Union of Myanmar), is a country in Southeast Asia. It borders Bangladesh and India to the west and northwest, the People's Republic of China to the northeast, Laos and Thailand to the east and southeast, and the Andaman Sea and the Bay of Bengal to the south and southwest. The capital city is Naypyidaw, and the commercial capital is Yangon. [1]\n\nThe earliest settlements in Myanmar were in the Pyu city-states and Mon kingdoms. [8] Around the 9th century AD, the Bamar people established the Bagan Kingdom near the Irrawaddy River, leading to the emergence of Bamar culture and language. The First Myanmar Empire, Bagan, fell due to Mongol invasions. In the 16th century, the Second Myanmar Empire, the Toungoo Empire, emerged and temporarily stood as the largest empire in Southeast Asian history. [9] In the 19th century, the Third Myanmar Empire, the Konbaung Empire, ruled not only the regions of present-day Myanmar but also Assam and Manipur. The British Empire invaded and conquered Myanmar through a series of wars. During World War II, Myanmar was briefly occupied by the Japanese and then recaptured by the Allies. In 1948, Myanmar gained independence from the British.\n\nMyanmar's post-independence history has been marked by ongoing instability and conflicts that continue to this day. The 1962 military coup led to a military dictatorship under the Burma Socialist Programme Party. Subsequently, the 8888 Uprising on August 8, 1988, led to a nominal transition to a multi-party system two years later, but the post-crisis military council refused to hand over power and continues to govern the country to this day. The country remains fractured by ethnic conflicts among its numerous ethnic groups and is facing one of the world's longest-running civil wars. The United Nations and many other organizations have reported continuous and systematic human rights violations within the country. [10] Following the 2010 general election, the military government was officially dissolved in 2011, and a nominal civilian government was established. Daw Aung San Suu Kyi and political prisoners were released, and the 2015 general election was held, leading to improved foreign relations and easing of economic sanctions. [11] However, the country's treatment of ethnic minorities, especially in relation to the Rohingya conflict, continued to be a source of international tension and concern. [12] After the 2020 Myanmar general election, in which Daw Aung San Suu Kyi's party won a majority in both houses of parliament, the Tatmadaw (military) once again seized power through a military coup. [13] The coup, widely condemned by the international community, led to widespread ongoing protests across Myanmar, marked by severe political repression by the military and an escalation of the civil war. [14] The Tatmadaw arrested Daw Aung San Suu Kyi to remove her from public view and charged her with various crimes, from corruption to violating COVID-19 regulations, all charges which independent observers claim are \"politically motivated.\" [15]\n\nMyanmar is a member of the East Asia Summit, the Non-Aligned Movement, ASEAN, and BIMSTEC, but despite once being part of the British Empire, it is not a member of the Commonwealth of Nations. Myanmar is a dialogue partner of the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation. The country is very rich in natural resources such as jade, gemstones, oil, natural gas, teak, and other minerals, and also possesses renewable energy potential with the highest solar energy potential compared to other countries in the Greater Mekong Subregion. [16] However, Myanmar has long suffered from instability, political violence, corruption, poor infrastructure, and a long history of colonial exploitation that disregarded human development. [17] In 2013, the country's GDP (nominal) was US$56.7 billion, and GDP (PPP) was US$221.5 billion. [18] Income inequality in Myanmar is one of the widest in the world, with a large portion of the economy controlled by the military regime's cronies. [19] Myanmar is one of the least developed countries. [20] Since 2021, the post-coup civil war has displaced over 600,000 people across Myanmar, and over three million people are in urgent need of humanitarian assistance. [21] According to the UNHCR, as of December 2024, there are over 1.3 million refugees and asylum seekers and 3.5 million internally displaced persons. [22]\n\nTerminology\n\nMain article: Names of Myanmar\n\nIn 1989, the military government decided to change \"Burma\" to \"Myanmar\" in English.\n\nWestern countries, not recognizing the military government, consider that it lacks the authority to officially change the country's name in English. [23] Therefore, while they accept \"Myanmar\" in Burmese, they continue to use \"Burma\" in English. When referred to in English, users choose between \"Burma\" or \"Myanmar,\" and technically, these terms are interchangeable.\n\nMajor international news agencies such as BBC, Wall Street Journal, and Financial Times, as well as the governments of the United States and Great Britain, continue to refer to Myanmar as Burma. However, CNN, The Economist, and The New York Times have adopted \"Myanmar\" as the term for the country and \"Burmese\" as the adjective. [24] [25]\n\nThe governments of Great Britain, Canada, Australia, and the United States continue to use Burma. [24] [26] However, the European Union uses both \"Burma/Myanmar\". [27] China does not use \"Myanmar\" but uses its traditional name \"Miǎndiàn\" (缅甸). Japan calls Myanmar \"Myanmar\" (ミャンマー) but continues to refer to its people as \"Burmese\" (ビルマ人). France, like most other countries, uses its traditional translation and calls Myanmar \"Birmanie\". [28] [29]\n\nFollowing the new government's ascension in 2015, Union Minister for Foreign Affairs Daw Aung San Suu Kyi met with ambassadors, military attachés, and consuls from various foreign embassies in Naypyidaw and stated that it does not matter whether the country is called Myanmar or Bama. [30]","usageMetadata":{"candidatesTokenCount":1371,"promptTokenCount":3062,"promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":3062}],"thoughtsTokenCount":239,"totalTokenCount":4672}}
