[01:27:58.913] Browser LOG     Frontend: Data received from API: {"model":"gemini-flash-lite-latest","source_language":"auto","target_language":"en","translated_text":"History of Myanmar\n\nMyanmar (English: Myanmar), officially the Republic of the Union of Myanmar (English: Republic of the Union of Myanmar), is a country in Southeast Asia. It borders Bangladesh and India to the west and northwest, the People's Republic of China to the northeast, and Laos and Thailand to the east and southeast. The Bay of Bengal and the Andaman Sea are located to the south and southwest. The capital of the country is Naypyidaw, and the commercial capital is Yangon.[1]\n\nThe earliest settlements in Myanmar were in the Pyu city-states and Mon kingdoms.[8] Around the 9th century AD, the Myanmar people established the Bagan Kingdom near the Ayeyarwady River, where Myanmar culture, the Myanmar language, and so on emerged. The First Myanmar Empire, the Bagan Kingdom, fell due to the Mongol invasion. In the 16th century, the Second Myanmar Empire, the Taungoo Empire, emerged and temporarily stood as the largest empire in Southeast Asian history.[9] In the 19th century, the Third Myanmar Empire, the Konbaung Empire, was able to rule the regions of present-day Myanmar as well as Assam and Manipur. The British Empire invaded and occupied Myanmar through wars of aggression. During the Second World War, Myanmar was temporarily occupied by the Japanese and then retaken by the Allied forces. In 1948, Myanmar gained independence from the British.\n\nThe post-independence history of Myanmar is filled with unrest and conflict that continues to this day. The 1962 military coup led to a military dictatorship under the Burma Socialist Programme Party. Subsequently, the August 8, 1988 Uprising led to a transition to a nominal multi-party system after two years, but the post-uprising military junta refused to hand over power and continues to rule the country to this day. The country is fragmented by ethnic conflicts among numerous ethnic groups and is facing one of the world's longest civil wars. The United Nations and many other organizations have reported ongoing and systematic human rights violations in the country.[10] Following the 2010 general election, the military government was formally dissolved in 2011, and a nominal civilian government was established. Aung San Suu Kyi and political prisoners were released, and the 2015 general election was held, leading to improved foreign relations and easing of economic sanctions,[11] but the country's treatment of ethnic minorities, particularly in connection with the Rohingya conflict, remained a source of international tension and concern.[12] Following the 2020 Myanmar general election, in which Aung San Suu Kyi's party won a majority in both houses of parliament, the Tatmadaw seized power again in a military coup.[13] The coup, which was widely condemned by the international community, led to widespread protests across Myanmar and was marked by the military's severe political repression and the escalation of the civil war.[14] The Tatmadaw arrested Aung San Suu Kyi to remove her from public view and charged her with crimes ranging from corruption to violating COVID-19 regulations; all charges against her are considered \"politically motivated\" by independent observers.[15]\n\nAlthough Myanmar is a member of the East Asia Summit, Non-Aligned Movement, ASEAN, and BIMSTEC, it is not a member of the Commonwealth of Nations, despite once being part of the British Empire. Myanmar is a dialogue partner of the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation. The country is very rich in natural resources such as jade, precious stones, oil, natural gas, teak, and other minerals, and possesses renewable energy potential with the highest solar energy potential compared to other countries in the Greater Mekong Subregion.[16] However, Myanmar has long suffered from instability, political violence, corruption, poor infrastructure, and a long history of colonial exploitation that did not prioritize human development.[17] In 2013, the country's nominal GDP was US$56.7 billion, and its GDP (PPP) was US$221.5 billion.[18] Income inequality in Myanmar is among the widest in the world, and a large portion of the economy is controlled by cronies of the military government.[19] Myanmar is one of the least developed countries.[20] Since the 2021 coup, the ongoing civil war has displaced over 600,000 people across Myanmar, with over three million people in urgent need of humanitarian aid.[21] According to the UN High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR), as of December 2024, there are over 1.3 million refugees and asylum seekers and 3.5 million internally displaced persons.[22]\n\nLexicology\n\nMain article: Names of Myanmar\n\nIn 1989, the military government decided to change the English name Burma to Myanmar.\n\nBecause Western countries do not recognize the military government, they consider it not to have the authority to officially change the country's name in English. Therefore, while they accept and use Myanmar in the Myanmar language, they continue to use Burma in English. When used in English, users choose between \"Burma\" or \"Myanmar,\" and the terms are technically interchangeable.\n\nMajor international news agencies such as the BBC, The Wall Street Journal, and the Financial Times, as well as the US and UK governments, continue to refer to the country as Burma, while CNN, The Economist, and The New York Times have adopted the term Myanmar for the country and Burmese as an adjective in English.[24][25]\n\nThe governments of Great Britain, Canada, Australia, and the United States continue to use Burma.[24][26] However, the European Union uses both Burma/Myanmar.[27] China does not use Myanmar but uses Myanma (缅甸) according to their customary naming. Japan calls the country Myanmar (ミャンマー) but continues to use Burmese (ビルマ人) for the Myanmar people. France, like most other countries, uses the traditional translation and calls the country Birmanie.[28][29]\n\nAfter the new government took office in 2015, Union Minister for Foreign Affairs Aung San Suu Kyi met with ambassadors, military attachés, and consuls from various foreign embassies based in the country in Naypyidaw and stated that it did not matter whether they called it Myanmar or Burma.[30]","usageMetadata":{"candidatesTokenCount":1331,"promptTokenCount":3062,"promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":3062}],"totalTokenCount":4393}}
